WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.858
SPEAKER: "Critter
Junction" has their eye

00:00:01.858 --> 00:00:03.450
on reducing cold start latency.

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.580
And that's going to take
a bit of detective work.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.800
Stay tuned to see how
they profile start up

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.621
times to speed things up.

00:00:09.621 --> 00:00:12.918
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:18.530
"Critter Junction's"
been seeing longer than

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:21.780
wanted startup times through
their Compute Engine instances

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.440
even though they set everything
according to our auto scaling

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:25.970
recommendations.

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:28.880
They know they were running
into some logic on their game

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.220
servers on Compute
Engine like taking

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:34.520
user inputs to spawn them
into a new Critter's island.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:36.890
But after profiling
their startup times,

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:39.980
they were seeing more
than 380 second cold start

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.950
times while the response
latency for a request

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.470
was in the 300
millisecond range.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:49.070
They also did a performance test
to see how long Compute Engine

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:52.520
was taking to create those
instances versus how much time

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.680
their code was taking to run.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:58.190
This showed request,
provision, and boot time

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:00.260
straight from the Cloud Shell.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:03.170
Request is the time
between asking for a VM

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:06.410
and getting a response back
from the Create Instance API

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:08.750
acknowledging that
you've asked for it.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:11.060
You can profile this
by timing how long

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:14.630
it takes Google Cloud to respond
to the insert instance rest

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:16.160
command.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.320
Provision is the time
that Compute Engine

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.770
takes to find space for
the VM on its architecture.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:24.620
Use the get instance
API on a regular basis

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:26.390
and wait for the
status flag to change

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:28.880
from provisioning to running.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.000
And boot time is when startup
scripts and other custom code

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.480
executes up to the point when
the instance is available.

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:37.550
Just repeatedly pull
a health check that

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:40.220
is served by the same
runtime as your app,

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.160
then time the change
between receiving 500, 400,

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.460
and 200 status codes.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:48.200
After doing these,
"Critter Junction"

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.690
noticed the majority
of instant startup time

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:53.450
usually happened
during the boot phase

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:56.180
when the instance
execute startup scripts.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:58.640
This is not uncommon, so
you should profile your boot

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:01.010
scripts to see which phases
are creating performance

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:02.600
bottlenecks.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.790
To get a sense of what
stages of your script

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:07.190
are taking the most
boot time, one trick

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:09.590
is to wrap each section
of your startup script

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:11.900
with the seconds
command, then append

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:14.810
the time elapsed for
each stage to a file,

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:18.770
and set up a new end point to
serve that file when requested.

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:21.380
This let "Critter
Junction" dig even deeper

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:23.630
to pull the end point
and get data back

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:26.480
without too much heavy
lifting or any modification

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.100
to their service.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:29.330
And there it is.

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:30.890
The performance
bottleneck seemed

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:34.160
to be public images,
preconfigured combinations

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:36.290
of the OS, and boot loaders.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:39.030
These images are great when
you want to get up and running.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.570
But as you start building
production level systems,

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:43.280
the large portion
of boot up time

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.010
is no longer booting
the OS but the user

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:49.370
executed startup sequence that
grabs packages and binaries

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:51.170
and initializes them.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:53.180
"Critter Junction" was
able to address this

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:56.390
by creating custom images for
their instances, which you can

00:02:56.390 --> 00:03:00.110
do from source disks, images,
snapshots, or images stored

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:01.700
in Cloud storage.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:05.330
Then use those images
to create VM instances.

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:07.070
When the target
instance is booted,

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:10.620
the image information is
copied right to the hard drive.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:12.620
This is great when you've
created and modified

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.180
a root persistent disk
to a certain state

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.770
and want to save that state
to reuse it with new instances

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:21.980
and when your setup includes
installing and compiling

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:24.560
big libraries or
pieces of software.

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:27.500
When you're trying to scale to
millions of requests per second

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:30.320
being serviced by
thousands of instances,

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:33.080
a small change in boot time can
make a really big difference

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:36.500
in costs, response time,
and most importantly,

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:39.230
the perception of
performance by your users.

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:41.930
Stay tuned for what's next
for "Critter Junction."

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:45.640
And remember, always
be architecting.

